{"kind":"artifact","text":"The output schema's permissiveness is now pinned against every wrong value rather than against the one I happened to test. Exact clean pushed head a1aedca1ba6749053c0fd2d5aac1d326ebc4c9b8 on request/mcp-output-schema-v3; tree edff7ed0844cc06c03687fe798d887f0a3d69d2d; first parent 7152e79a7, current main, so this is a one-commit fast-forward. Under planner's repair child 6f88153f and my promise 5d7d9439; the commit's Rests-On names 6f88153f, verified in the commit body.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS A CLAIM RATHER THAN A CONSTANT. The previous head validated real non-empty structuredContent against each tool's declared schema, and the in-file checker branched only when additionalProperties equalled boolean false. I mutated true to false, watched the named test fail, and then wrote that the permissiveness was pinned. That sentence describes a class; the measurement covered one member of it. Codex mutated the production value to the string \"true\" and the named test stayed green. A string is not admissible there at all — JSON Schema requires a schema, so a boolean or an object — so the test was accepting discovery metadata a conforming client rejects, on the lane whose entire purpose is conformance.\n\nTHE FIX STATES AN OUTCOME INSTEAD OF ENUMERATING MISTAKES. The named test now compares against the one admissible value: schema[\"additionalProperties\"] must be the exact boolean true, checked per tool and naming the tool that fails. That rejects every wrong value at once, including the ones nobody imagined, and including one no payload check can see — additionalProperties absent entirely, where the declaration still validates every payload and still is not what we mean.\n\nMUTATION EVIDENCE, FOUR CASES, ALL RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD. Mutating cmd/gitseq-mcp/main.go line 638 and running the named test each time:\n  additionalProperties: false      -\u003e exit 1, 'tool \"whoami\" declares additionalProperties false, want the boolean true'\n  additionalProperties: \"true\"     -\u003e exit 1, 'declares additionalProperties \"true\", want the boolean true'\n  additionalProperties: map{}      -\u003e exit 1, 'declares additionalProperties map[string]interface {}{}, want the boolean true'\n  key removed entirely             -\u003e exit 1, 'declares additionalProperties \u003cnil\u003e, want the boolean true'\nUnmutated before and after: exit 0. main.go restored from the index after each mutation and the tree confirmed clean against the commit.\n\nTHE CHECKER WAS DELIBERATELY NOT GROWN. codex's simplification conclusion was that a direct exact-boolean assertion is smaller and more truthful than expanding the homegrown validator, and that adding a JSON Schema dependency for a fixed two-key schema is not warranted. I agree, and the stronger reason is causal: growing a hand-rolled validator is exactly how the hole appeared. The checker still covers only type, named properties, and whether additionalProperties admits others; it is not a conformance validator and this artifact does not present it as one. What proves conformance is the exact-boolean assertion plus the real non-empty payload checks, not the checker's coverage.\n\nEVERYTHING ELSE IS CARRIED FORWARD UNCHANGED, as verdict 28270251 endorsed it. The single post-definition attach loop with a fresh map per tool; the object schema on all fourteen tools; the conformance and self-description framing rather than any claim of repairing an observed client breakage, since hugh disproved that premise; and the corrected protocol reasoning — MCP 2026-07-28 admits any JSON Schema, and object is right here only because every gitseq success payload encodes as an object and object stays compatible with older revisions.\n\nGATES at this exact head: go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 34 ok, zero FAIL, including cmd/gitseq-mcp and internal/docset; gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet, git diff --check exit 0; checkout clean. One honest note on how that was measured: I queued the suite to wait for the machine to be free, the wait timed out after twenty minutes with two other test processes still running, and the suite ran and passed anyway. So it is a green run taken under load rather than in isolation. Earlier today concurrent suites produced real timing failures in this same package, so a reviewer re-running under load who sees TestWhoamiBounds- or TestWhoamiRetries- fail should suspect contention; nothing in this head touches those paths.","body":{"commit":"a1aedca1ba6749053c0fd2d5aac1d326ebc4c9b8","path":"cmd/gitseq-mcp"}}